NEW DELHI: The Indian government has urged WhatsApp to take immediate action to stop the spread of hot messages on its app, which has recently sparked a spate of violent incidents. Subsequent occurrences. This is according to a quote from the BBC on Thursday afternoon, July 5, 2018.
The request comes amid a spate of deadly violence that has killed at least 17 people across India in the past three months. All of this violence has been blamed on child abduction rumors that have been widely circulated on WhatsApp, leading to a spate of violent attacks on strangers. .
Indian police say it is "very difficult" to convince people that the messages being distributed are untrue. One of the latest incidents of violence took place in the northeastern state of Tripura, where the victim was a man hired by the local government to campaign in the villages to ask locals not to leave. There is a belief in the rumors that are being shared on social media.
