The full text of the petition, originally written in French with the signatures of Cambodians, reads as follows: Minister of Commerce explains Smart Asiata about the terms of registration of trademarks in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): In order to contribute to strengthening the implementation of the national intellectual property sector, Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, explained to Smart Axiata, a mobile telecommunications operator, about the trademark law and some requirements for obtaining a company name registration. And the right to protect legitimate trademarks in Cambodia.
His Excellency's interpretation was made during a meeting with Mr. Thomas HUNDT, CEO of Smart at the Ministry of Commerce on the morning of July 4, 2018, who applied for the registration of Smart Asiataco brand. Ltd. and registration as a well-known brand.
On that occasion, Mr. Pan Sosak informed Mr. Thomas Hun about the difference between a trademark and a public word, as well as the necessary conditions for choosing a name to register a trademark. The Minister requested the CEO of Smart Asiata to cooperate with the Department of Intellectual Property of the Ministry of Commerce to meet the conditions under the Trademark Law and the act of dishonest competition as part of the intellectual property sector.
He also highlighted the Ministry of Commerce's commitment to supporting the private sector and the promotion of the national intellectual property sector, and thanked Thomas Hun for his investment and social activities. Cambodia.
In response, Mr. Thomas Hun thanked the Minister for explaining the procedures and requirements for trademark registration in Cambodia. He promised to prepare a request for re-registration of the company's trademark in collaboration with the Department of Intellectual Property and to comply with the statutes set by law.
Please note that a trademark refers to a name or mark used to identify and differentiate a particular company or enterprise. The use of trademarks in Cambodia shall comply with the Law on Trademarks and Acts of Dishonest Competition, which was approved by the National Assembly and adopted on December 6, 2001 at the 7th Session of the 2nd National Assembly.
