(Phnom Penh): A delegation from the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia of the Ministry of Economy and Finance led by HE Dr. Kun Nhim, Director General of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia attended the 132nd Annual Meeting of the World Customs Organization from June 28-30, 2018 in Brussels, Belgium.
The meeting focused on a number of key topics, including: Trade facilitation focuses on the implementation of the WTO trade facilitation agreement. E-Commerce and the Challenges of Customs Administration Prevention against illegal financing 4. Cooperation between customs and police in preventing transnational crime; and 5. Human resource building, institutional development and governance.
At the same time, the meeting also heard reports and discussed on customs technical work, such as rules of origin, tariffs and classification of goods, compliance and trade facilitation and capacity building. Among the key tools of the WCO, the Conference also adopted a standard framework on cross-border e-commerce to help member countries develop e-commerce management strategies and plans.
A delegation from the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia also took part in a bilateral meeting with a Thai Customs delegation led by Director General Kulit Sombatsiri to strengthen cooperation between the two customs administrations and discuss the requirements to expedite the signing. On the Memorandum of Understanding on Transit Goods between the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Kingdom of Thailand.
Following the meeting of the World Customs Organization, the Cambodian Customs delegation proceeded to attend the 20th Meeting of the French-speaking Customs Administration, which took place from 2 to 3 July 2018 in Lille, France. .
The conference focused on strengthening customs cooperation, including the exchange and sharing of experiences and the promotion of the use of the French language in the field of customs. Representatives of the Cambodian Customs Delegation also made presentations on the experience and progress of the reform and modernization of the Cambodian Customs Institution, especially on the implementation of the ASYCUDA system, E-Customs and the one-stop-shop system.
