Australia and other nations opposed to whaling are protesting against Japan as Tokyo seeks to resume whaling trade and fishing by 2018. This was released by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Tuesday evening, July 3, 2018.
Japanese officials have acknowledged that their country is seeking support and re-approval for whaling at a meeting of the International Whaling Commission (IWC) in Brazil in September. This face. But at the same time, the Australian Ministry of Environment and Energy has said it will try to prevent any further Japanese attempts to catch whales.
"Australia will categorically oppose any proposal to change whaling restrictions." According to the Australian Ministry of Environment and Energy.
Apart from Australia, New Zealand, most countries in Europe, South America and Central America have also expressed opposition to Japan's whaling plans. 333 whales were killed by Japan during annual fishing in the Antarctic Ocean last winter. Of the 333 whales, 128 were female and 122 were pregnant.
