Director of Phnom Penh Land Management Department receives complaints from people to Senior Minister Chea Sophara
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sok Oeun, who claims to be the legal owner of a land in Sen Sok district, complained to Mr. Sarin Vanna, Director of the Department of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction of Phnom Penh to Senior Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, who made a distorted report. Really deceive the leadership.
Mr. Sok Oeun, 64 years old, said on July 3, 2018 that he had sued Mr. Sarin Vanna for abusing and abusing his superiors, requesting the Ministry of Land Management to inspect Mr. Sarin's actions. Vanna, who made a report to distort the truth and deceive Senior Minister Chea Sophara according to the report No. 505 Don Sos / Rop dated April 19, 2017 and the officials who conspired with each other.
According to Mr. Sok Oeun, he owns a 90-by-45-meter plot of land located on Street 1978, Phnom Penh Thmey Village and Poung Peay Village, Sangkat Phnom Penh Thmey, Khan Sen Sok, and sold 2,850 square meters of land to Sok San and his wife. Jakha has made a clear contract in order to avoid disputes: each party has to pay 5 meters of their land for the construction of Road 1978.
After the party received the title with a thumbprint, Sok San refused to comply with the contract to leave his land 5 meters to build a road because he conspired with cadastral officials to turn the road to all of Mr. Sok Oeun. Make Mr. Oeun Toch's land impossible to build a house. (Attach claim file)
Subsequently, the Ministry of Land Management, through letter No. 3392, dated December 29, 2017, decided for Mr. Lim Vann, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Land Management to conduct a detailed inspection with the Inspectorate and the Department of Land Management in Phnom Penh. Suddenly, on May 28, 2018, Phnom Penh Capital Hall met about this case and reached Sen Sok District Hall to inform about the demolition of buildings and fences on Mr. Sok Oeun's land.
Mr. Sok Oeun expressed his doubts about the action of Phnom Penh Capital Hall, which ignored the letter from the Ministry of Land Management appointing Mr. Lim Vann, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Land Management to examine this case. No decision has been made yet, and suddenly the legal construction of Mr. Sok Oeun is required.
Finally, the legal landowner requested Senior Minister Chea Sophara to send an inspection team to investigate Sarin Vanna and his cronies in connection with the irregularities in Report No. 505 DONSOR / RP dated 19 April. 2017 Disrespect for the higher hierarchy, abuse of power at the top, and a meeting to resolve the issue without Mr. Sok Oeun's participation, as well as a request for sanctions to suspend the work according to the seriousness of this serious mistake.
The temporary opening ceremony of the railway from Pursat to Bat Doeng was presided over by Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport.
The section of the railway from Phnom Penh to Serei Sophorn, Banteay Meanchey province, was reopened after the overthrow of the Pol Pot regime, but was canceled in 2006 due to deterioration.
In particular, the section of the railway from Serei Sophorn to Poipet has been disrupted since the 1970s due to the weight of bombs in 1973 and has not been used since the overthrow of the Pol Pot regime.
After the war was completely extinguished and the current transport needs were met, the section was rehabilitated and put into operation. The railway section from Poipet to Serei Sophorn, nearly 48 kilometers long, was officially opened on the morning of April 4, 2018.
A 65-kilometer section of railway from Serei Sophorn to Battambang City was opened on April 29, 2018.
The 107-kilometer road from Battambang to Pursat will be opened on May 29, 2018. Separately, the section of railway from Pursat to Phnom Penh will be put into temporary use today.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol had said that the rehabilitation of the railway from Phnom Penh to Poipet, Banteay Meanchey province, cost about 96.6 million US dollars. Of that, about $ 13 million belongs to the Asian Development Bank and another $ 83.6 million is from the Royal Government.
