With the support of the Supreme People's Court of China, the Commercial Court will be launched later this month to handle arbitration and trade and investment disputes arising among the countries involved in the project. Rings and roads, the name of China's ambitious development program for international trade and infrastructure.
"Our dispute resolution mechanism incorporates good options such as arbitration and litigation to become one," said Liao Kusiang, a judge of the Supreme People's Court of the People's Republic of China. Therefore, this court gives more options to the parties involved. "They can choose to file a complaint to the International Trade Court or to the arbitral tribunal, which is the choice of both parties, or they can choose to mediate."
He said mediations would be conducted by international legal experts with an in-depth understanding of international trade and international investment law.
"Members of the expert committee will be tasked by the International Trade Court to mediate disputes, interpret international trade rules and provide advice on access to foreign laws that may apply to such disputes," he said. "The Chinese Supreme People's Court is currently working to establish rules for a committee of experts on international trade affairs."
The first International Commercial Court will be set up in Xinjiang, in the southern Chinese province of Guangdong, and the second in Xi'an, northwest China's Shaanxi Province.
So far, the Chinese Supreme People's Court has approved legal interpretations related to the International Trade Court, defining the types of cases that the ICC will accept and clarifying the proceedings of the adjudication mechanism. (China's CCTV)
