Under international law, Antarctica is not ruled by any country, but in a 1959 treaty of 45 signatories, the continent opened its doors to scientific research and conservation. It is also the suspension of previous claims by seven countries on the continent: Argentina, Australia, Chile, France, New Zealand and the United Kingdom.
So it means that if any country has an interest or possibility in visiting and setting up a research base on the planet, they can ask for permission from the relevant international organizations, as Turkey did a few months ago. Interested in going to study on this ice continent.
In addition, if a country or company wants to travel to visit the continent for research or recreation (requires an icebreaker), they do not need to apply for a visa or visa from any country. Stopping at any country on the way there, they certainly need to complete the procedures according to the laws of those countries.
Antarctica is located at the South Pole of the planet, with 98% of its surface area covered by ice up to 1.6 km or 2 km thick. It accumulates 90 percent of all ice on Earth and up to 70 percent of fresh water, which is quite large.
Even more shocking is that if the continent melted all the ice, the Earth's sea level would rise by another 60 meters. Considering the area of the continent, up to 14 million square kilometers (composed of ancient stone or mostly marble), which is close to the size of Australia and Brazil combined. For the people living on the continent (researchers) there are only about 1,000.
