(Phnom Penh): PassApp and Wing customers can easily travel around Phnom Penh after the two companies signed a memorandum of understanding this morning, July 2, 2018 to provide means of payment for travel. Tuk-tuk, convenient and fast.
Speaking at the event, Jojo Malolos, CEO of Wing Specialized Bank, said that the partnership will provide a new opportunity for thousands of people living in Phnom Penh and using Wing services to enhance their daily lives. They have a new experience of traveling without cash around the city.
Top Nimol, CEO of PassApp Technology, said: "This partnership is part of the company's commitment to ensuring that our customers are able to adapt to the new age of technology in their entire travel experience, including From booking a tuk tuk in just a few minutes and paying for travel with the push of a button.
"We are also developing the ability of our tuk-tuk drivers to manage their finances," said Top Nimol. Once the travel fee is credited to the Wing account, they will be able to access the daily earnings report. "In addition, some drivers will be more comfortable because they do not have to worry about safety when driving a tuk-tuk to customers or traveling home at night."
Customers can also top up their accounts at Wing agents nationwide. With Wing account, customers have many options for cashless payment such as bill payment, online shopping, mobile top-up, money transfer and more.
On Monday morning, according to the Thai Navy's Facebook page, Thai authorities began pumping air into 351 new oxygen supply pipelines, which were sent there with the help of the military. Thais. In addition to the existing 415 oxygen tubes, a total of 766 oxygen tubes will be filled with air, and this will take 12 hours.
These oxygen tubes are fed into the third section of the cave above for the divers to use in their efforts to reach a fragment where young children live. All 12, as well as one of their football coaches, are believed to be trapped there.
