BEIJING: The world's second-largest economy is on track to develop into one of the top countries in space, amid both the technology and the frequency of satellite launches by an Asian giant. This is growing at a rapid pace, which should be remarkable. This is according to a quote from the Russian newspaper Sputnik on Saturday evening, June 30, 2018.
China became the fifth country in the world to launch a satellite into space in 1970. So far, a total of 400 satellites have been launched and more than 200 are currently in operation. A large number of satellites have been developed in China, covering the fields of communications, meteorology, navigation and space science.
Since 2010, China has been constantly working to increase the number and frequency of its satellite launches. In general, about 20 satellites are launched each year by China.
The Dongfanghong-4 satellite is a milestone in the history of China's satellite development. When China was building this large-scale communications satellite in 1999, there were many proposals to buy technology from abroad, but modern technology is not affordable. Therefore, Chinese scientists have been actively working to innovate and develop independent technologies on their own.
China is one of the few countries in the world to have advanced space technology and to build and develop space systems.
