According to the World Health Organization, traffic accidents kill more than 260,000 Chinese people a year. In the eastern Chinese city of Hangzhou, authorities are trying to reduce the death toll by using security cameras.
"We are all very careful when traveling today," said Suu Kyi, a company employee. They have multiple cameras connected to the AI virtual intelligence system. Wrong driving will be recorded and reported immediately. "So the traffic order in Hangzhou is much better."
There are hundreds of traffic lights and security cameras throughout the city. Chinese tech giant Alibaba has spent two years collaborating with traffic police on an Internet data storage system called "City Brain" to make cameras smart enough to recognize or broadcast. Irregular activities that occur on the streets.
With live video capability, the city's brain system can report potential accidents in advance, confirm an accident at any location within 20 seconds, and can adjust traffic lights to allow ambulances. Can pass through traffic.
"Our technology will enable the cameras to automatically detect the situation on the road," said Hour Xiansoeng, senior director of Alibaba Group. "Currently, the level of clear information provided by this system is at least 95 percent."
